WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:00.870
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Hello.

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:01.980
My name's Dustin Kirkland.

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.647
I'm a product manager
at Google, and I'm

00:00:03.647 --> 00:00:05.430
joined here by Lukas Heinrich.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.530
He's a physicist at CERN and
has a pretty exciting experiment

00:00:10.530 --> 00:00:13.260
that he reproduced
using cloud technology.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.250
And he's going to tell
us a bit about that.

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:17.083
LUKAS HEINRICH: Yeah,
so during the keynote,

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.360
we rediscovered the Higgs boson
using a publicly available data

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.000
set.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.360
And during a keynote, we
launched 25,000 workloads

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.450
on the public cloud to
analyze collision data

00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:29.310
of the Large Hadron Collider.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.320
And there, we
selected events where

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.560
we had a pretty good chance that
the Higgs boson was originally

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.350
there.

00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:38.110
And during the keynote, you
could see a chart building up

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:39.540
where, in the
middle of the chart,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.400
there was a small peak forming.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.100
And that peak, more or
less, proved the existence

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:44.892
of the Higgs boson.

00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:46.392
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
That's fascinating.

00:00:46.392 --> 00:00:47.850
So you work on
the ATLAS project.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.970
And with the ATLAS project,
you're working on some data

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:51.940
from CERN.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:54.840
And I understand this has
some parallels to what we do

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.270
in the open source community.

00:00:56.270 --> 00:00:57.187
LUKAS HEINRICH: Right.

00:00:57.187 --> 00:00:59.040
So many of the
experiments at CERN

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.190
have an open data policy,
which means that data

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:05.010
that we record, we
release it openly.

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:06.670
So I work with
the ATLAS project,

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:09.600
but the data that
we used in the demo

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:11.850
was from the CMS project,
which is a project that

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.380
is very similar to mine.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:16.080
And it kind of
exemplifies that even

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.580
me, who is not a member
of the CMS project,

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.510
I can analyze this data from
them, because they released it

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.260
openly.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.170
And in fact, thanks to
these cloud resources,

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:26.910
anybody can do it.

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.850
One of the challenges in open
data for these large scale

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:31.980
scientific
collaborations, is always

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.040
that the scale of the
data is very large.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.020
And while we can release
it, it was always

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.990
a bit of a question of who
can practically analyze it

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.820
who doesn't have a data center.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:43.987
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And how
large is practically large?

00:01:43.987 --> 00:01:46.070
LUKAS HEINRICH: The data
set we analyzed yesterday

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:48.390
in the span of a few
minutes was 70 terabytes.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:49.170
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: 70 terabytes.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.462
It's not even practical for
me to download that and try

00:01:51.462 --> 00:01:54.180
to store that at home on
a laptop in a university.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.260
LUKAS HEINRICH: Exactly.

00:01:55.260 --> 00:01:57.963
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: But this can
be found now in the cloud.

00:01:57.963 --> 00:01:58.880
LUKAS HEINRICH: Right.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:01.195
So we have it available
at CERN, the open data.

00:02:01.195 --> 00:02:03.570
And then we transfer it,
because we have high speed links

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:05.170
to the cloud providers.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.290
We could transfer it into
Google Cloud Storage.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.780
And this is from where we
read the data to analyze it.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.267
And now anybody could,
in principle, do that.

00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:14.350
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: So a
student, another university,

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.520
another organization,
anyone can work on that data

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.683
without downloading it.

00:02:18.683 --> 00:02:19.600
LUKAS HEINRICH: Right.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.850
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: And where
do you see this going next?

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.740
Give us some idea of how
these methods and Google Cloud

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.168
can be used for your research.

00:02:28.168 --> 00:02:30.210
LUKAS HEINRICH: So I think
what's super exciting,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:32.910
is that this open data
originally oftentimes

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:36.510
was seen as something for
outreach or educational

00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.990
purposes that you have
really small scale data sets,

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:43.620
but now researchers are
starting to really use it

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.060
for real scientific applications
outside of the experiments.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:50.880
So people who have nothing to do
with the Large Hadron Collider,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.490
they can analyze this data
that we release openly.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.823
I think that is pretty exciting.

00:02:54.823 --> 00:02:55.740
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Cool.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.973
And what's your next project?

00:02:57.973 --> 00:03:00.390
LUKAS HEINRICH: So one thing
that I really want to try out

00:03:00.390 --> 00:03:03.970
in the cloud ecosystem is
functions-as-a-service.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:08.648
So this is something that kind
of fits well onto our work

00:03:08.648 --> 00:03:09.690
that we showed yesterday.

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.770
And so we'll maybe get
back home and try that out.

00:03:12.770 --> 00:03:14.700
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
Cloud Run, Knative,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.720
on top of Kubernetes.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:16.950
LUKAS HEINRICH: Exactly.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:17.520
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Tell me a little bit

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:18.892
about your next project.

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:20.100
What are you working on next?

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.893
And how do you see
using Google Cloud?

00:03:21.893 --> 00:03:23.310
LUKAS HEINRICH:
So I think what is

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.450
pretty exciting is the release
of Cloud Run a couple of weeks

00:03:26.450 --> 00:03:26.950
ago.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.820
And so the workload that
we showed yesterday during

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:32.580
the demo actually would
fit nicely into this

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.265
functions-as-a-service paradigm.

00:03:34.265 --> 00:03:35.640
And maybe when we
get home, we'll

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:38.270
try this out and see whether we
can run the demo in this way.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.020
DUSTIN KIRKLAND: Yeah,
that's fascinating.

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:43.450
I would imagine those
batch-type services really match

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.700
to the work that you're doing.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:46.742
LUKAS HEINRICH: It's all
the data transformations

00:03:46.742 --> 00:03:48.990
where, so the data that we
download is in one form,

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:52.750
and then we process it and
produce a summary format.

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:54.418
And so this is
essentially a function.

00:03:54.418 --> 00:03:55.835
DUSTIN KIRKLAND:
Thank you, Lukas.

00:03:55.835 --> 00:03:56.793
I appreciate your time.

00:03:56.793 --> 00:03:59.840
[MUSIC PLAYING]

